  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nCurrently we have an upper level trough moving across the Four \nCorners region and bringing with it some clouds and rain showers. \nThe showers are confined to the Gila Region and most of the clouds\nare west of the Rio Grande. The upper level trough will continue \nto lift into Colorado and take the clouds and rain with it. \nWednesday will start off partly cloudy but by late morning we will\nhave mostly sunny skies with high temperatures running near \nseasonal averages. For Thursday and Friday, another upper level \ntrough will begin to organize along the California coast. Out \nahead the trough we will see a dry southwest flow develop across \nthe region. This pattern will keep us dry and bump our high \ntemperatures a few degrees above average. \n\nOn Saturday the next upper level trough will move across northern\nArizona and New Mexico. This system will be too far north to give\nus a chance for precipitation, but as the trough passes by on \nSaturday, we will see some breezy winds. High temperatures both \nSaturday and Sunday will run a few degrees below average. On \nMonday the yet another upper level trough will push into New \nMexico. Some models keeps us dry and some models give us a decent \namount of rain (and maybe mountain snow). Right now I have limited\nthe the rain and snow to area mountains. It is is way to early to\nbe talking about any snow accumulations, but right now they look \non the light side of things. There are still lots of time for the \nmodels to shift the precipitation amounts (or get rid of them all \ntogether). As you might expect high temperatures on next Monday \nand Tuesday will run 3 to 5 degrees below average. Still no sign \nof a strong push of cold air into the lowlands through \nThanksgiving.",
